.TH TERMNAV-RELAY 1
.SH NAME
termnav-relay \- route navigation through nested SSH and tmux scopes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B termnav-relay send
.I pane direction
[\fB--client-pid\fR \fIPID\fR]
.br
.B termnav-relay send
.I window next|previous
[\fB--client-pid\fR \fIPID\fR]
.br
.B termnav-relay send
.I move left|right
[\fB--client-pid\fR \fIPID\fR]
.br
.B termnav-relay commit --tmux-socket \fIsocket\fR --client-tty \fItty\fR --client-pid \fIpid\fR --client-created \fIseconds\fR
.br
.B termnav-relay navigate
.I action direction --parent client-options
.br
.B termnav-relay warm
.br
.B termnav-relay stop
.br
.B termnav-relay ssh
[\fIssh arguments\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Termnav establishes one owner-only Unix socket forwarding channel on each
interactive SSH connection. Navigation requests are handled by the nearest
tmux scope and otherwise forwarded to its parent. The protocol contains only
fixed pane and window navigation operations.
.PP
Interactive shell activation starts one owner-only dispatcher under
\fB$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/termnav-$UID\fR. The hot \fBsend\fR, \fBcommit\fR, and
tmux-only \fBnavigate\fR forms reuse that process instead of starting a Python
interpreter for each keypress. \fBwarm\fR starts it asynchronously; \fBstop\fR
shuts it down. It also exits after one hour without a request. Unsupported or
unusual argument forms retain the ordinary command parser, and transport
failures remain fail-closed so a gesture is never executed twice.
.PP
Install \fBshare/termnav/sshd_config\fR as an sshd_config.d fragment on any
host that should participate, validate with \fBsshd -t\fR, and reload sshd.
.PP
Explicit SSH remote commands are eligible when their command line requests a
TTY. Termnav preserves the destination and remote command as their original
OpenSSH arguments. Commands without that explicit TTY request, configured
remote commands, and non-session control modes remain ordinary SSH.
.PP
For an eligible interactive login or commanded TTY, Termnav exports
the current relay path in the invoking client process and requests
\fBSendEnv=TERMNAV_PARENT_RELAY\fR. OpenSSH carries that value per session even
when it reuses a ControlMaster, so concurrent sessions retain distinct relay
sockets without a second connection or authentication attempt.
.PP
Termnav never changes the remote command channel or OpenSSH's normal login-shell
selection. Native Windows and other non-POSIX targets therefore continue to
receive the original command or interactive shell even when they cannot create
the remote Unix-socket forward. An effective \fBExitOnForwardFailure=yes\fR
policy is delegated unchanged without adding the optional relay. On other
unsupported servers OpenSSH may report a nonfatal remote-forward warning; the
session itself continues normally. Failure to receive usable relay metadata
makes navigation a silent no-op.
.SH TWO-PHASE COMMIT TRANSPORT
Navigation uses a two-phase commit. The executing hop first prepares a
per-nonce, per-client directive under
\fB$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/termnav-$UID/directives/<hash>/\fR, then recursively
prepares every outward tmux hop. Only after the complete path exists does the
commit phase make those directives claimable and write the read-only
DECRQM query \fBESC [ ? 2004 $ p\fR to the originating terminal. Its ordered
reply is one of \fBESC [ ? 2004 ; 0..4 $ y\fR; tmux \fBuser-keys[9]\fR through
\fBuser-keys[13]\fR bind all five legal states to \fBtermnav-relay commit\fR.
No terminal extension or terminal-specific OSC handler is required.
When no Termnav directive is pending, the binding passes the response back to
the originating pane for compatibility with tmux versions that do not answer
application DECRQM queries themselves.
.PP
When another tmux layer lies between the terminal and the executing hop, its
commit forwards the private in-band key \fBESC [777009u\fR
(\fBuser-keys[8]\fR) into the nested client. Each commit claims the sole
prepared directive for that physical client and either forwards again or
performs the pane/window action. Window and move scopes use stable-ID
swap logic; pane scope uses a single guarded \fBif-shell\fR (edge check and
\fBselect-pane\fR atomically) to avoid TOCTOU.
.PP
At every request, a tmux-backed relay resolves the live client displaying its
source pane. A sole client wins, then a unique focused client, then a unique
recent activity timestamp; ambiguity fails closed. This deliberately avoids
pinning client identity when the long-lived SSH process starts, because a
persistent pane can later be driven from another terminal. A tmux-less SSH hop
instead retains its own stable launch-time tty and PID.
.PP
Directives carry only the client identity selected at that hop, including
tmux's client creation stamp; origin client fields are never forwarded between
hosts. Commit reruns the same conservative client-selection policy immediately
before acting, so a focus, pane, session, detach, or PID-reuse race fails
closed. Parent relay discovery likewise reads the selected client's current
environment for each request.
.PP
Normal prepare failures explicitly abort every outward hop. A pending
directive is reclaimed after a 30 s crash-recovery lease only when its caller
cannot complete that rollback. Ready directives have no persisted expiry, so
arbitrary nesting depth cannot consume their validity while commit walks back
inward. Each relay waits up to 6 s for its own completion receipt after the
outer call returns. Publication, sequence allocation, claiming, and completion
receipts use the per-socket
\fB.directives.lock\fR plus owner-only atomic files. A writer crash therefore
cannot expose partial state, and a malformed record is removed without acting.
Because DECRQM responses do not carry a nonce, at most one directive may be
outstanding for a physical tmux client across all relay servers. \fBsend\fR
returns success only after the matching commit receipt is durable.
.PP
There is no watchdog direct-execution fallback. If the terminal does not answer
DECRQM, the exact directive is removed and that still-attached tmux client is
poisoned. Later requests from the same attachment fail closed instead of
letting a delayed response execute an unrelated action. Detaching and creating
a new tmux client establishes a fresh identity.
.SH EXIT STATUS
\fBtermnav-relay send\fR exits \fB0\fR when handled, \fB3\fR when no local,
parent, or terminal endpoint owns the request, \fB1\fR on transport or other
error (fail-closed, do nothing), and \fB2\fR on usage error.
\fBtermnav-relay commit\fR always exits \fB0\fR (silent, bounded runtime) —
it is invoked from a foreground tmux \fBrun-shell\fR so client input pauses
until the switch lands. Stray commits with no pending directive are no-ops.
.PP
At the top hop, tab actions continue through the same terminal dispatch used by
local routing. Once a parent relay is present, its timeout or transport failure
is an error and never permits a second fallback path.
.PP
\fBsend\fR itself never mutates tmux; only \fBcommit\fR does.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERMNAV_RELAY_LOG
When set, the relay server appends debug lines (\fBbegin\fR/\fBend\fR per
nonce) to the given path.
.SH RESIDUAL RISKS
Replies the terminal emits \fIafter\fR the commit barrier for queries from the
departing pane, and post-switch handshakes from the departing subtree, can
still misroute — the same exposure the pre-relay design had (never observed in
practice). Fixing those requires reply routing inside tmux itself. Remote
\fB/tmp/termnav-relay-<token>.sock\fR
forward path portability (Termux/no-/tmp) is a deferred known limitation —
local artifacts already use \fB$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fR, remote \fBRemoteForward\fR
stays \fB/tmp\fR for v2. \fBps eww\fR fallback remains best-effort and
fail-closed (not a robust parser).
